Why are properties not logged in the order they were defined?
In JavaScript, when using for...in or Object.keys() on objects, numeric string keys (like "1", "2", "100") are sorted in ascending numerical order, regardless of the order you added them. Non-numeric string keys retain their insertion order.

This is why your object’s properties may appear out of order when logged.

for...in Behavior with Numeric Keys
The for...in loop:

Enumerates all enumerable properties, including inherited ones.

Orders numeric keys first (sorted numerically).

Then, includes string keys (in insertion order).

📦 Object.keys() Behavior
Object.keys(obj):

Returns an array of an object’s own enumerable string keys.

The order:

Numeric keys sorted numerically.

Then, string keys in insertion order.

So in this regard, it's very similar to for...in, except it only includes own properties, not inherited ones.

💡 Example: Mixed Key Object

const mixedKeys = {
  "b": "string key",
  "2": "numeric string key",
  "a": "another string key",
  "1": "another numeric string",
  "100": "larger numeric string"
};

// for...in iteration
console.log("for...in output:");
for (const key in mixedKeys) {
  console.log(key);
}

// Object.keys() output
console.log("Object.keys() output:");
console.log(Object.keys(mixedKeys));



Why This Happens:
1, 2, 100 are numeric keys → sorted numerically first.

Then b, a are string keys → shown in insertion order.